# DESCRIPTION

The time service sets the system's UTC.
On amd64 it reads the PC's real-time clock once, as it starts, and publishes that time in the UTC page the launcher gave it to write; every process maps the page, and `clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME)` reads it.
On a devicetree machine (rv64's) the clock is a driver's: devmgr(8) mounts a driver whose tree has an `rtc` at `dev/rtc0`, such as p1(3)'s, the Orange Pi RV2's PMIC.
The service waits for it on a thread of its own, up to 20 s, while its tree already answers, then publishes the date it reads there.
Without a clock UTC stays at the launcher's starting value; on arm64 there is none to read.

The clock is read through the CMOS ports 0x70 and 0x71.
The service waits out an update in progress, then reads the registers again until two readings agree (at most six).
It takes the registers as the clock's status register says they are kept, binary or BCD and a 24-hour or 12-hour clock, and the year from the century register, 0x32, where there is one and as 20YY where there is not.
The machine's clock is taken to be UTC.
The time is published with an error of one second, the clock's resolution.
A reading that is no date (a month 13, a 30th of February) is refused.

The manifest (manifest(6)) of a machine that has the clock:

```text
service time
program time
export
early
clock
resource ioport
```

`early` starts it before the services that read the time, `clock` gives it the UTC page to write and `resource ioport` the ports.
A devicetree machine's uses devmgr's tree instead, and isn't `early`, as devmgr starts its drivers first:

```text
service time
program time
export
clock
use devmgr
mount /mnt/devmgr devmgr
```

A driver's clock reads and is written as one line, `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`, UTC.
The service prints a line to the log when it has set the time, and exits with an error, saying why, if it has no page, no ports, a clock that says no date, or cannot write the page.

# EXAMPLES

On a machine whose clock reads 9:15:30 on 12 October 2026, reading `/svc/time/status` gives a line like this:

```rc
% cat /svc/time/status
set from rtc 2026-10-12T09:15:30Z utc 1791796530000000000
```

# FILES

: `/svc/time/status`
  What the service did: on amd64, `set from rtc `, the date it read as `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`, ` utc ` and the same time as nanoseconds since 1970; on a devicetree machine `set from /mnt/devmgr/dev/rtc0/rtc ` and the same; `no rtc` without a clock.
  A line, ended by a newline.
: `/svc/time/ctl`
  `set YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`: a driver's clock set to that date, then UTC published from it, as when the service started.
  A date that isn't one, a machine whose clock isn't a driver's (amd64's), or a clock that refuses it fails the write as invalid.
  A cpu(8) session has the time service at `/svc/time`, so the terminal can set the board's clock: `echo set 2026-11-02T02:17:10Z >/svc/time/ctl`.

