# BUGS

It reads the clock once, and again only after a `set`.
UTC drifts from the clock afterwards, and the service never writes the clock back on its own.
Nothing sets the clock from the network: `ctl`'s `set` is the only way.
On the Orange Pi RV2 the clock loses its time with the board unplugged (p1(3)): UTC after such a boot is the year 2000's until a `set`.
arm64's machines have no clock it reads; there `status` says `no rtc`.
The amd64 clock can't be set through `ctl`.
